#: src/3808-register-tool.md:35
msgid "Why support custom lints?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:37
msgid ""
"There are several crates, such as `bevy` and `regex`, that would benefit "
"from API-specific lints that encourage specific styles or warn against "
"potential footguns. While it is possible to create a custom `rustc` driver "
"that registers these lints, any reference to them in code would cause the "
"default compiler to raise an error."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:40
msgid ""
"// While `bevy_lint` will recognize this, the default `rustc` will not, "
"raising a compile error.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:44
msgid ""
"There are currently two solutions to this: [upstream lints directly to "
"Clippy](https://rust-lang.github.io/rust-clippy/master/index."
"html#invalid_regex) or [use `#[cfg_attr(my_tool, warn(...))]`](https://"
"thebevyflock.github.io/bevy_cli/bevy_lint/index.html#toggling-lints-in-"
"code). The prior solution increases the maintenance burden for Clippy "
"developers, and thus will rarely be accepted. The latter is very verbose and "
"requires adding `unexpected_cfgs = { level = \"warn\", check-cfg = "
"[\"cfg(my_tool)\"] }` in `Cargo.toml`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:46
msgid ""
"There are also several linting tools that don't make sense to upstream to "
"Clippy:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:48
msgid ""
"[`cargo-semver-checks`](https://github.com/obi1kenobi/cargo-semver-checks/) "
"(uses its own analysis framework, unrelated to `rustc_driver`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:49
msgid ""
"[`dylint`](https://github.com/trailofbits/dylint) (custom, user-extensible "
"lints)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:50
msgid ""
"[`marker`](https://github.com/rust-marker/marker) (custom, user-extensible "
"lints, but a different approach)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:51
msgid ""
"[`klint`](https://github.com/Rust-for-Linux/linux/pull/958) (Rust-for-Linux "
"specific linter)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:53
msgid "Why support custom attributes?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:55
msgid ""
"There are also some tools that would benefit from using developer-added "
"metadata on portions of source code:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:57
msgid ""
"Formal verification tools, such as [prusti](https://viperproject.github.io/"
"prusti-dev/user-guide/syntax.html) and [kani](https://github.com/model-"
"checking/kani), want to mark specific functions for verification. While "
"adding contracts is an [explicit project goal](https://rust-lang.github.io/"
"rust-project-goals/2024h2/Contracts-and-invariants.html), there are many "
"existing tools for contracts that developers would benefit from in the "
"meantime, and formal verification includes more than just contracts."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:58
msgid ""
"Coverage tools, such as [tarpaulin](https://github.com/xd009642/tarpaulin/"
"#ignoring-code-in-files), allow marking specific functions as skipped."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:59
msgid ""
"Source code translation tools, such as [c2rust](https://github.com/immunant/"
"c2rust/blob/d28087df86d7fca8532d8679d35efec66f074f8b/c2rust-refactor/tests/"
"reorganize_definitions/old.rs#L18), want to mark the origin of generated "
"code. One could imagine this also being useful for any kind of generated "
"code, such as a build script, for recording [Source Map](https://web.dev/"
"articles/source-maps)\\-like metadata."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:69
msgid "Guide-level explanation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:72
msgid "For users of external tools"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:74
msgid ""
"Several official tools let you configure their behavior on specific parts of "
"your code. For example, Clippy lets you use `#[warn(clippy::"
"as_ptr_cast_mut)]` to warn on that lint for a single item, and Rustfmt lets "
"you use `#[rustfmt::skip]` to avoid formatting a single item. You can also "
"do this for external tools that are not provided in the Rust toolchain. See "
"the documentation of those tools for the lints and attributes they support."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:76
msgid ""
"To tell the compiler about an external tool, add `#!"
"[register_tool(some_tool)]` to your crate root."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:78
msgid ""
"Crate-level lints for external tools can use `#![warn(some_tool::"
"lint_name)]`, like any lint. Tools may also support a custom configuration "
"format that allows you to control lints for your whole workspace at once. "
"Consult the documentation of the tool you use."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:82
msgid "Fixing name resolution errors"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:84
msgid ""
"Note that `register_tool` changes name resolution, and may give errors if "
"you have a crate named `some_tool`. The compiler will suggest ways to fix "
"the new errors."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:87
msgid ""
"If a tool name conflicts with a crate name, you can disambiguate the crate "
"with `::some_tool`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:92
msgid "//~ ERROR: is this the tool or a proc-macro?\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:94 src/3808-register-tool.md:99
#: src/3808-register-tool.md:110
msgid "// ...\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:97
msgid "// OK: This is the proc-macro defined in the crate.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:102
msgid ""
"However, if you want to go on to use a tool attribute, you must rename the "
"crate so it doesn't conflict:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:108
msgid "// OK: This is the attribute specified by the tool.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:113
msgid ""
"Alternatively, if you only want to use lints, you can use "
"`register_lint_tool` instead of `register_tool`, which will avoid resolution "
"errors."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:115
msgid "Overlaps like this are expected to be rare in practice."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:121
msgid "For authors of external tools"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:123
msgid ""
"The Rust language can be extended and analyzed using external tools. If your "
"tool can parse Rust, you may wish to allow configuring it at sub-crate "
"levels (e.g. individual functions, types, and modules). To reuse the same "
"syntax as the official tools, like Clippy and Rustfmt, instruct your users "
"to add `#![register_lint_tool(your_tool)]` (if your tool only adds new "
"lints) or `#![register_attribute_tool(your_tool)]` (if your tool only adds "
"new attributes). If your tool supports both lints and attributes, use `#!"
"[register_tool(your_tool]`. Then, instruct your users to add either "
"`#[warn(your_tool::your_lint)]` or `#[your_tool::"
"your_attribute(your_tokens)]` as appropriate."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:125
msgid ""
"We do not specify a syntax for package-level configuration. We suggest using "
"`[package.metadata.your_tool]` in Cargo.toml."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:127
msgid ""
"The syntax for external attributes is carefully designed such that you do "
"not need to do name resolution in order to recognize the attributes. As long "
"as `register_attribute_tool(your_tool)` is present at the crate root, "
"`#[your_tool::your_attribute]` will always be an [inert](https://rustc-dev-"
"guide.rust-lang.org/attributes.html#builtininert-attributes) attribute you "
"can parse directly; it can never be a re-export of a different item, nor a "
"reference to a local item."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:129
msgid ""
"Please _do_ verify that `register_attribute_tool` is present, and either "
"warn or error otherwise. If you do not do so, you may accidentally interpret "
"a crate or local module as your tool. We will ensure that `rustdoc --output-"
"format json` includes `register_attribute_tool` so that users of rustdoc "
"json are not required to reimplement a rust parser."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:132
msgid ""
"Please do _not_ suggest using `#[cfg_attr(your_tool, your_attribute)]`. "
"Doing so runs the risk that the language will add that lint or attribute in "
"a future version. Use tool namespaces instead, as that's what they're for! "
"It's ok to pass a custom `cfg` when your tool runs, but avoid using it to "
"guard tool lints and attributes unless it would break your MSRV (minimum "
"supported Rust version)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:134
msgid ""
"Please do _not_ use tool attributes for metadata that changes the meaning of "
"the code. At that point you are parsing a dialect of Rust, and there is no "
"indication for your users that their code will be interpreted differently by "
"your tool than by the compiler.  For example, `#[must_use]` and "
"`#[automatically_derived]` would be suitable for tool attributes, but "
"`#[repr]` and `#[panic_handler]` are not, because they change the meaning of "
"the code. For that use case, use proc-macros, generated code, or bare (un-"
"namespaced) attributes instead, all of which will give a hard error if they "
"cannot be understood by the compiler. If absolutely necessary to use bare "
"attributes, use a C-style namespace like `#[rustc_const_stable]`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:136
msgid "Reference-level explanation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:139
msgid "Language"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:141
msgid "Background: name resolution of preludes"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:143
msgid ""
"Currently, names in the type namespace are resolved in [the following order]"
"(https://github.com/rust-lang/reference/pull/1765):"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:145
msgid "Explicit definitions (including imports)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:146
msgid ""
"[Extern prelude](https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/reference/names/preludes."
"html#extern-prelude) (crates injected using either `extern crate` or `--"
"extern`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:147
msgid ""
"[Tool prelude](https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/reference/attributes."
"html#tool-attributes)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:148
msgid "Standard library prelude"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:149
msgid "Language prelude"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:153
msgid ""
"Note that currently, loading a crate completely prevents using a tool "
"attribute with that name. Consider this program:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:156
msgid "// or --extern rustfmt\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:158
msgid "// ERROR could not find `skip` in `rustfmt`\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:162
msgid "Semantics"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:164
msgid ""
"The tool prelude is separated into the tool attribute prelude (which is in "
"the type namespace) and the lint prelude (which is only active inside lint "
"controls)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:166
msgid ""
"The `#![register_attribute_tool(ident)]` crate-level attribute adds a new "
"tool to the tool attribute prelude. The `#![register_lint_tool(ident)]` "
"crate-level attribute adds a new tool to the lint prelude. For both "
"attributes, tools must be a single ident, not a nested path."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:168
msgid ""
"`register_attribute_tool` and `register_lint_tool` are idempotent; "
"duplicating the attribute has no effect."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:170
msgid ""
"Crate-level macros such as `#![cfg_attr(...)]` may expand to "
"`register_*_tool`, subject to all existing rules for macro expansion."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:172
msgid ""
"Like today, attributes and lints in a tool namespace are always considered "
"used by the compiler. The compiler does not verify the contents of any tool "
"attribute, except to verify that all attributes are syntactically valid "
"[tool attributes](https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/reference/attributes."
"html#tool-attributes)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:174
msgid ""
"Registering a predefined tool (`clippy`, `miri`, etc.) using `#!"
"[register_*_tool(...)]` is an error."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:176
msgid ""
"The `rustc` tool namespace is currently reserved and will continue to be "
"reserved after this RFC, i.e, `register_*_tool(rustc)` is an error."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:178
msgid ""
"Note that the compiler currently reserves all attributes starting with "
"`rustc` (such as `#[rustcat]`), even if they do not have a trailing `_`. "
"That continues to be the case after this RFC, but tool names starting with "
"`rustc` are not explicitly prohibited in `register_*_tool` attributes."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:181
msgid ""
"Ambiguity between a tool name and any other name in the type namespace is "
"always a hard error. For example, this code would error:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:187 src/3808-register-tool.md:197
msgid "// ERROR: ambiguous\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:188 src/3808-register-tool.md:199
msgid "// OK\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:193
msgid "// Import the derive macro.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:198
msgid "// ERROR: ambiguous (even though y is not present)\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:203
msgid ""
"This is in order to not require external tools to perform name resolution. "
"This restriction may be relaxed in the future to favor tool names."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:205
msgid ""
"To disambiguate a local item, developers may use `self::` to force "
"resolution in the current module. To disambiguate a crate, developers may "
"write `#[::rustfmt::skip]`, which forces resolution in the [Extern prelude]"
"(https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/reference/names/preludes.html#extern-"
"prelude). To disambiguate an item in the standard prelude (e.g. `Clone`), "
"developers may use `std::prelude::v1::Clone`. There is no way to "
"disambiguate the tool; developers must use `extern crate rustfmt as "
"my_rustfmt` (or the equivalent in Cargo.toml) to avoid the ambiguity. As a "
"quality of implementation issue, errors should mention one of the above "
"workarounds."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:213
msgid ""
"Modules in the first path of an attribute (e.g. `#[unregistered::name]`) are "
"assumed to be a crate if they cannot be resolved, and therefore give a hard "
"error if not registered."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:215
msgid ""
"Unknown tool names in lints remain a hard error until the story for proc-"
"macro lints is resolved (see [Future possibilities](#future-possibilities))."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:217
msgid ""
"[Inner attributes](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/54726) for tools "
"remain a hard error. Tool names are not affected by the current scope, but "
"we need to know the current scope in order to give an ambiguity error."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:221
msgid ""
"Today, `#![no_implicit_prelude]` suppresses the tool attribute prelude but "
"does not suppress the lint prelude (e.g., `#[allow(clippy::foo)]` continues "
"to work). Under this RFC, neither prelude is suppressed by `#!"
"[no_implicit_prelude]`. This is technically a breaking change since it can "
"produce new ambiguity errors in stable code."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:231
msgid "Drawbacks"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:234
msgid "This makes the rules for name resolution even more complicated."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:236
msgid ""
"This runs the risk that external tools will add attributes that change the "
"meaning of the code, such that the behavior is different when the tool is "
"present. There is not much we can do about this other than to ask tool "
"authors not to do that."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:238
msgid ""
"This introduces a new \"meta-breaking\" concern: once this is stabilized, "
"adding a new tool namespace, like we did for `diagnostic` and "
"`rust_analyzer`, becomes a breaking change. That said, this is what editions "
"are for; I think adding a new namespace is rare enough that waiting for an "
"edition isn't a big deal in practice."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:240
msgid ""
"The lang team [expressed a concern](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/"
"issues/66079#issuecomment-1010266282) in 2022 that the name "
"`register_*_tool` would mislead users into thinking that this "
"_automatically_ runs the tool. I do not think this is likely in practice; if "
"someone adds this attribute, it's because the docs for the external tool "
"told them to do so, and those docs should also say how to run the tool."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:244
msgid "Rationale and alternatives"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:247
msgid ""
"We could \"just not do this\". That makes it harder to write external tools, "
"and in practice just means that people use `cfg_attr` instead of a "
"namespace, which seems strictly worse."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:248
msgid ""
"We could relax the constraint that tool names cannot conflict with local "
"items. This requires tools to do name resolution; but in practice I do not "
"think we can expect tools to do this, and we must assume that the tool will "
"behave differently than the compiler (`rustfmt` already does this today)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:249
msgid ""
"We could change name resolution so tools take precedence over crate names, "
"instead of giving an ambiguity error. That would allow using crates side-by-"
"side with tools of the same name; but it would complicate the name "
"resolution rules."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:250
msgid ""
"We could add a syntax to disambiguate tool names from local items. That "
"would add inconsistency with the existing built-in tool attributes, and "
"requires tool authors to parse both the new and existing syntax."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:251
msgid ""
"We could use a CLI argument for register_tool instead of a crate-level "
"attribute. This means that the source code is no longer independent from the "
"way it's built (although this is already true for some existing flags, like "
"`--edition`). This would also be unnecessary if [`--crate-attr`](https://"
"github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/3791) is merged, since that would allow "
"passing any attribute as a flag."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:252
msgid ""
"We could rename the attribute from `register_*_tool` to something else; "
"perhaps `import_*_tool`, `inject_*_tool`, or `use_*_tool` by analogy with "
"the `use` keyword. However, that makes the semantics unclear, and emphasizes "
"the [lang concern](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/"
"issues/66079#issuecomment-1010266282) about it seeming as if the tool is run "
"automatically. `rustc` calls adding new lints \"registering\" internally, "
"and I think this is a good name for the semantics."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:253
msgid ""
"We could continue using a single `register_tool` attribute instead of "
"splitting it up into `register_lint_tool` and `register_attribute_tool`. "
"This is slightly less complicated to write, but has the drawback that merely "
"adding a lint tool changes name resolution for attribute macros, even if the "
"tool does not define any attribute."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:254
msgid ""
"`register_attribute_tool` could be named `register_metadata_tool` instead. "
"`register_metadata_tool` makes it clear that tool attributes do not change "
"the meaning of code, but `register_attribute_tool` makes it clearer how the "
"tool is intended to be used."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:258
msgid "Prior art"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:261
msgid ""
"[`clang-tidy`](https://clang.llvm.org/extra/clang-tidy/#suppressing-"
"undesired-diagnostics), [`pylint`](https://pylint.pycqa.org/en/latest/"
"user_guide/messages/message_control.html#block-disables), [`eslint`](https://"
"eslint.org/docs/latest/use/configure/rules#using-configuration-comments-1), "
"and [`review`](https://github.com/Bogdanp/racket-review#usage) (a racket "
"linter) use inline comments. Whether these count as namespacing is "
"debatable; pylint and eslint include their name in the inline comment and "
"clang-tidy does not. `review` allows both `review: ignore` and `lint: "
"ignore`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:262
msgid ""
"[Roslyn analyzers](https://johnnyreilly.com/eslint-your-csharp-in-vs-code-"
"with-roslyn-analyzers#deactivate-linting-partially) and [gcc](https://gcc."
"gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Diagnostic-Pragmas.html) use `#pragma`s. GCC uses "
"`#pragma GCC` and Roslyn uses `#pragma warning`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:263
msgid ""
"C and C++ use [vendor attributes](https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/"
"attributes), which are very similar to tool attributes, including "
"namespacing. They do not have syntactic ambiguity with items in the type "
"namespace and so do not perform any kind of name resolution; in Rust terms, "
"all vendor attributes are [inert](https://rustc-dev-guide.rust-lang.org/"
"attributes.html#builtininert-attributes). Like this RFC, and unlike the "
"current language, C++ mandates that tools do not restrict namespaces they "
"don't recognize."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:264
msgid ""
"C# uses [attributes](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/"
"attributes/applying-attributes), which are [active](https://rustc-dev-guide."
"rust-lang.org/attributes.html#non-builtinactive-attributes), not [inert]"
"(https://rustc-dev-guide.rust-lang.org/attributes.html#builtininert-"
"attributes), i.e. they follow normal name resolution rules. Like attribute "
"macros in Rust, they can be defined in user code and are unrelated to "
"external tools."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:265
msgid ""
"[`Resyntax`](https://docs.racket-lang.org/resyntax/"
"Refactoring_Rules_and_Suites.html#(part._.Exercising_.Fine_.Control_.Over_."
"Comments)) (a racket refactoring tool, like `cargo fix`) does not allow "
"inline configuration; instead it requires you to write an extension to the "
"tool specifying the new behavior in code."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:278
msgid "Unresolved questions"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:281
msgid ""
"How does this interact with [proc-macro lints](https://github.com/rust-lang/"
"rust/pull/135432)?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:283
msgid "Future possibilities"
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:286
msgid ""
"We could allow registering tools in Cargo.toml (with a `package.tools` or "
"`workspace.tools` field). This would avoid duplicating tool registration for "
"each crate in the package/workspace. This depends on [`--crate-attr`]"
"(https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/3791) being stabilized."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:287
msgid ""
"If this is a `package.tools` field, it should allow workspace inheritance."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:288
msgid ""
"External tools often have a dedicated cfg (e.g. `cfg(kani)`). We could add a "
"way for registering the tool to also register the name with `check-cfg`. We "
"would need an opt-out for tools that don't have a dedicated cfg."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:289
msgid ""
"Since tool authors will usually be different from package authors, we may "
"want to allow specifying tool metadata in a reusable form (e.g. `.cargo/"
"tools.toml`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:290
msgid ""
"We could allow building an external tool as a [runnable dependency](https://"
"github.com/rust-lang/cargo/issues/2267)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:291
msgid ""
"We could make `[lints]` support external tools as a first-class feature. "
"This needs a way for tools to read the metadata out of `Cargo.toml` (e.g. "
"`cargo metadata`, or just parsing the toml file), because cargo does not "
"drive external tools. Additionally, we cannot guarantee that tools will "
"actually read the metadata."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:292
msgid ""
"`[lints]` could automatically imply registering the tool, even if not "
"present in `package.tools`."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:293
msgid ""
"Proc macros wish to register custom lints; see [`proc_macro_lint`](https://"
"github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/135432). We would have to establish some "
"mechanism to prevent overlapping namespaces. Perhaps `warn(::project::"
"lint_name)` could refer to the proc macro and `warn(project::lint_name)` "
"would refer to any registered tool (only when a `project` tool is "
"registered; in the common case where no tool is registered, `project::` "
"would still refer to the proc macro)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:294
msgid ""
"Projects may wish to have both a proc-macro crate with lints and a CLI with "
"lints. To allow this, we would require `proc_macro_lint` to create an "
"exhaustive list of lints that can be created, such that we can still run "
"`unknown_lints` and do not need to create a new cooperation mechanism "
"between `proc_macro_lint` and `register_lint_tool`, nor to require users of "
"the project to distinguish the two with `::project` (see immediately above). "
"We might still run into difficulty if the proc-macro lint namespace is only "
"active while the proc-macro is expanding; it depends on how "
"`proc_macro_lint` is specified. But I think it's ok to delay that discussion "
"until `proc_macro_lint` gets an RFC."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:295
msgid ""
"We could allow attribute macros to register a [derive helper](https://doc."
"rust-lang.org/nightly/reference/procedural-macros.html#derive-macro-helper-"
"attributes), so that they can emit other attributes for an external tool."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:296
msgid ""
"We could allow proc-macros to register a scoped tool, such that e.g. "
"`#[serde::flatten]` is valid while the proc-macro is expanding, but not "
"elsewhere in the crate. This is similar to [derive helpers](https://doc.rust-"
"lang.org/nightly/reference/procedural-macros.html#derive-macro-helper-"
"attributes), but namespaced. We would have to take care to avoid ambiguity "
"between the scoped tool and globally registered tools in such a way that "
"external tools still do not need to perform name resolution."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:297
msgid ""
"Once [expression attributes](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/15701) "
"are stabilized, this would also allow tool attributes on expressions."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:298
msgid ""
"Some existing attributes, such as [`coverage`](https://doc.rust-lang.org/"
"nightly/unstable-book/language-features/coverage-attribute."
"html#coverage_attribute), have exactly the semantics of a tool attribute: "
"they add additional meaning when a specific feature or flag is enabled, and "
"ignored otherwise. They could use this mechanism (over an edition boundary, "
"as described above)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:299
msgid ""
"We can allow defining and exporting tool attributes from crates using "
"declarative macro syntax, which would enable IDE tooling to provide "
"suggestions."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:300
msgid ""
"This would require tools to do name resolution, which is a drawback. "
"However, the compiler can provide an extension point akin to rustdoc JSON "
"that exports tool attributes as metadata."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:301
msgid ""
"Taking advantage of this would require users to move away from register_tool "
"and use a crate dependency with the name of the tool instead. This would be "
"a benefit for users who already depend on a support crate from their tool, "
"like [crubit_annotate](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/"
"pull/3808#issuecomment-2866000525), as they would no longer also need "
"register_tool macros at their own crate root."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:302
msgid ""
"One danger of accepting this proposal now is that tools will use the crate "
"name for their binary instead of saving it for an eventual support library "
"that can declare attribute macros and potentially other things. This could "
"be mitigated with explicit guidance to use another crate name. For example, "
"kani uses [kani-verifier](https://crates.io/crates/kani-verifier) for the "
"binary while reserving [kani](https://crates.io/crates/kani) for an eventual "
"library."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:303
msgid ""
"This is somewhat akin to the way the [proc_macro_lint](https://github.com/"
"rust-lang/rust/pull/135432) proposal puts declared lints in the macro "
"namespace."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:304
msgid ""
"We can allow defining and exporting tool attributes from crates using Rust "
"types. This would enable the use of versioned, structured metadata that can "
"be shared among different tools and reflection APIs."
msgstr ""

#: src/3808-register-tool.md:305
msgid ""
"This could be combined with attribute macros to present more flexible syntax "
"options."
msgstr ""
